<!-- question: 169584 -->
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Exponential Family - Gamma Link</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The density function for the Gamma distribution is given by:</p>
<p>\[ f(y; \mu, \phi) = \frac{1}{\Gamma(1/\phi)} \left( \frac{1}{\phi\mu} \right)^{1/\phi} y^{1/\phi - 1} \exp \left( - \frac{y}{\phi\mu} \right) \]</p>
<p>By rewriting this in the exponential family form, identify the <strong>canonical link function</strong> \(g(\mu)\).</p>
<p>\(g(\mu) = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>We rewrite the density in the exponential form:</p>
<p>\[ f(y; \mu, \phi) = \exp \left\{ -\frac{y}{\phi\mu} + \left( \frac{1}{\phi} - 1 \right) \log y - \log \Gamma(1/\phi) - \frac{1}{\phi} \log(\phi\mu) \right\} \]</p>
<p>The term involving both \(y\) and the parameter \(\mu\) is \(-\frac{y}{\phi\mu}\).</p>
<p>In the exponential family form \( \exp \left( \frac{y\theta - b(\theta)}{a(\phi)} + c(y, \phi) \right) \), we identify \(\theta\) (the natural parameter) such that \(y\theta\) corresponds to the \(y\) term.</p>
<p>Here, the coefficient of \(y\) is \(-\frac{1}{\phi\mu}\). If we set \(a(\phi) = \phi\), then \(\theta = -\frac{1}{\mu}\).</p>
<p>The canonical link function \(g(\mu)\) relates the mean \(\mu\) to the canonical parameter \(\theta\). Thus, \(g(\mu) = \frac{1}{\mu}\) (or \(-\frac{1}{\mu}\)).</p>
<p>Commonly, the reciprocal link \(g(\mu) = 1/\mu\) is used.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
